118
00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:25.379
David Millar-Durrant: So… what speeds up this whole process, because that's kind of the aim of this talk, is,

119
00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:35.120
David Millar-Durrant: Something I realized is that Fieldline Bytes is actually a strict subset of a relatively mature, serialization and deserialization format called Borsch.

120
00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:52.800
David Millar-Durrant: Due to an incredibly beautiful, coincidence, they're both little Endian, they're both zero padding, there's no padding between fields, it's just a concatenated, set of bytes in declaration order, and it's fixed-sized byte arrays in raw bytes. So, it is a perfect superset.

121
00:14:52.820 --> 00:15:02.570
David Millar-Durrant: Of Borsch, now, of, FAB. Now, Borscht has extra things, like it has vectors, it has strings, it has variable width options, but if you just ignore that.

122
00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:14.230
David Millar-Durrant: They are byte-identical. And you're probably thinking, they're not byte-identical. Well, they're byte-identical for every single data type that persistent hash guarantees compatibility over. So that means you can switch over to Borsch.

123
00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:18.029
David Millar-Durrant: And you can retain the compatibility guarantees of persistent hash.

124
00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:21.459
David Millar-Durrant: Whilst moving over to a much more mature and documented format.

125
00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:26.650
David Millar-Durrant: Prove it, because this is a big claim. Here is a,

126
00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:38.429
David Millar-Durrant: big, chunky struct, which has most, data types you have around there. You have nesting, you have vectors, you have ethers, you have fields, and…

127
00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:40.849
David Millar-Durrant: That is equivalent to this Rust code.

128
00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:49.190
David Millar-Durrant: Using a standard Borsch serialize. Now, you can't exactly use standard library things, so you can't use a standard…

129
00:15:49.240 --> 00:16:06.290
David Millar-Durrant: Maybe you can't use a standard either, because you have to make sure that it's encoded in such a way where it uses a fixed number of bytes. So if you have two different size, things on the either, you have to pick the larger of the two things and then serialize it into the same number of bytes. Same with the option, you have to have a tag, which is, your U16.

130
00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:18.969
David Millar-Durrant: Which says, basically, which one you're going to use, and again, it's the size of the largest one. But that's a standard Borsch serialization thing. It spits out a schema, which is a standard Borsch schema type.

131
00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:33.130
David Millar-Durrant: And that schema will, actually give you exactly identical bytes and exactly an identical hash, for all data, data types, for all data types which are supported by a persistent hash. Beautiful coincidence. You don't have to write a spec.

132
00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:52.870
David Millar-Durrant: So some caveats in there, which are the ones I just said, and yeah, you can't use things exactly how you'd like, but you can describe them in such a way that everyone can understand it. And that's actually a really sort of beneficial thing here, because you have a well-defined setsback that you're a subset of, so it's much quicker to define that subset than it is to define a whole new format.

133
00:16:53.040 --> 00:17:06.239
David Millar-Durrant: Lots and lots of implementations already exist for this in lots of different languages. Pretty much every major language has a high-performance serializer and deserializer for this scheme. You know, not completely important, but very nice if you're trying to write a

134
00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:15.189
